- A simple FAQ.
- Transparent pricing.
- A contact/help section.
- A separate family-information page explaining optional assistance and privacy.
- No fabricated user numbers, reviews, endorsements, or success claims.

Make the experience feel dignified, safe, and welcoming rather than clinical or charity-driven.

### 15. TECHNICAL ARCHITECTURE

Use a maintainable, scalable architecture suitable for an Indian consumer application.

Preferred approach:
- Mobile-first responsive frontend, with a clear path to Android and iOS apps.
- A mature authentication system supporting OTP and secure account recovery.
- PostgreSQL or an equivalent relational database.
- Row-level security or equivalent server-side access control.
- Real-time communication through a reputable voice/video service or a production-ready WebRTC implementation.
- A secure backend for matching, moderation, subscriptions, and AI orchestration.
- An AI provider abstraction so models can be changed without rewriting the application.
- Speech-to-text and text-to-speech services for supported Indian languages.
- Secure push notifications with opt-in controls.
- Monitoring, error tracking, backups, and operational logging.
- A deployment setup with separate development, staging, and production environments.

Use environment variables and a secret manager for credentials. Never expose private API keys in frontend code.

Use real database persistence. Do not use hardcoded users, simulated verification, mock successful calls, or fake AI responses in production flows.

Document provider dependencies, expected operating costs, rate limits, and fallback behaviour when services fail.

### 16. CORE DATA MODEL

Create suitable database structures for:
- User accounts and authentication.
- User profiles and preferences.
- Language preferences.
- Verification status and verification audit records.
- Consent records and privacy settings.
- Friend requests, connections, and blocked users.
- Call sessions and connection metadata.
- AI conversation sessions and user-controlled memories.
- Groups, events, memberships, and attendance.
- Reports, moderation cases, and enforcement actions.
- Subscription plans, entitlements, billing records, and cancellations.
- Notifications and notification preferences.
- Audit logs for sensitive administrative actions.

Apply least-privilege access, data minimisation, encryption in transit and at rest, retention limits, and secure deletion workflows.

Keep face-verification data separate from public profile data. Avoid storing raw biometric material unless essential and legally approved.

### 17. ADMINISTRATION AND OPERATIONS

Build a secure, role-based admin dashboard.

Include:
- Account and verification review.
- User reports and moderation queues.
- Group and event management.
- Language and translation management.
- Subscription and billing oversight.
- Technical health and communication-provider status.
- Abuse and fraud indicators.
- Aggregate engagement and retention metrics.
- User support tickets.
- Data deletion and privacy request workflows.
- Audit trails for administrator actions.

Limit sensitive-data access to authorised roles. Prefer aggregate analytics and avoid exposing private conversation content to administrators by default.

### 18. LEGAL AND PRIVACY READINESS FOR INDIA

Design the product for compliance review under applicable Indian privacy and technology laws, including the Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 and the Digital Personal Data Protection Rules, 2025, taking account of the applicable commencement and enforcement timelines.

Before launch:
- Obtain qualified legal review of privacy notices, consent flows, age assurance, biometric processing, AI disclosures, platform responsibilities, and subscription terms.
- Implement accessible privacy notices and meaningful consent.
- Provide clear processes for access, correction, deletion, grievance handling, and withdrawal of consent as applicable.
- Define data retention and deletion schedules.
- Assess whether cross-border data transfers and third-party AI or communications providers meet applicable requirements.
- Establish incident response, breach handling, and vendor risk management procedures.
- Do not claim legal compliance until the implementation has been reviewed and verified.

### 19. PRODUCT ANALYTICS AND IMPACT

Measure whether the application creates meaningful, safe connections.

Track privacy-conscious aggregate metrics such as:
- Registration completion rate.
- Time taken to complete onboarding.
- Successful first AI conversation.
- Successful first human connection.
- Call connection success and failure rates.
- Repeat voluntary conversations.
- Meaningful connections sustained over time.
- Participation in group activities.
- User-reported sense of connection and ease of use.
- User satisfaction and accessibility issues.
- Reports, safety incidents, and moderation response times.
- Free-to-paid conversion, churn, and subscription cancellations.

Make reduction in self-reported loneliness an exploratory outcome, not a guaranteed product claim. Do not infer mental health diagnoses from engagement data.

Do not optimise for screen time alone. Reward meaningful, voluntary connections, user satisfaction, and safety.

